# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class
*.egg-info/
build/
dist/
.eggs/
.python-version

# Virtual environments
.venv/
venv/
env/

# uv
.uv/

# Tooling caches
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.coverage
.coverage.*
htmlcov/

# Environments and secrets
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Editors and OS
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
*.swo
.DS_Store

# Eval and trajectory artifacts
evals/trajectories/*
!evals/trajectories/.gitkeep
evals/briefings/*
!evals/briefings/.gitkeep
# evals/traces/ — the append-only cross-invocation trace log. NOT a
# refreshable pointer, despite sitting next to them: for a scenario
# killed before its per-scenario archive slice is written, this file is
# the ONLY record that the work happened and was billed. Proven on
# 2026-08-11, when saga_stuck died mid-scenario and existed nowhere
# else. Tracked so `git clean -fdx` cannot erase paid evidence
# (invariant 9: an artifact that can erase its own history cannot be
# used as evidence).
# These four families are VERSIONED, never overwritten
# (`<scenario>.<stamp>.<invocation_id>.<ext>`; evals/artifacts.py). The
# directory-wide patterns above and below cover the versioned names as well
# as the legacy flat ones, which stay on disk as evidence and stay ignored.
# `evals/reports/` itself is NOT an ignored directory — it holds the tracked
# baseline.json — so the versioned `report.<stamp>.<invocation_id>.json`
# files land there untracked and VISIBLE. That is deliberate and left to a
# human: no ignore rule is added for them, so committing a report is a
# decision someone makes, not one this file makes for them. The line below
# keeps the legacy `latest.json` ignored where it still exists.
evals/reports/latest.json
# Versioned successors of latest.json (cmd #185): one per run, never overwritten, durable copy
# lives in evals/runs/<id>/report.json. Ignored for the same reason latest.json was.
# Two patterns for one family: the flat name they used to have, and
# `evals/reports/runs/<YYYY-MM>/` where WO-R3-257 files them now. Note this is
# evals/REPORTS/runs — not `evals/runs/`, the per-invocation archive, which is
# deliberately tracked (see the block near the end of this file).
evals/reports/report.*.json
evals/reports/runs/
# Per-scenario folders since WO-R3-257 (`human/<scenario>/`, plus
# `human/_superseded/<scenario>/` for repeat renders). A directory-wide `*`
# excludes the sub-directories too, so one pattern still covers the family.
evals/reports/human/*
!evals/reports/human/.gitkeep
# `make world-dossier` output (cmd: evals/dossier.py). One versioned markdown
# file per pre-run reading of the fault world. Ignored, like the other
# per-run outputs: the dossier's job is to be READ before the spend and
# pasted into the readiness note, so the durable copy is the readiness note
# and the run archive, not a file that would add a few hundred lines to the
# repo every time somebody checks a world for free.
evals/reports/dossiers/*
!evals/reports/dossiers/.gitkeep

# The chaos-teardown latch (ADR 0037, evals/runner.py). Operational state
# with two transitions — a teardown compensator failed; an operator reset the
# world — and per-checkout by definition. Ignored so it can never be committed
# as if it were a fact about the repo; the durable record of the same event is
# the run's own report row, which IS committed with the archive.
evals/.chaos-teardown-block.json

# Claude Code runtime artifacts (per-machine)
.claude/

# evals/runs/ — the per-invocation immutable run archive — is the
# CLAUDE.md invariant-9 durable record and is deliberately NOT ignored:
# live-campaign archives must be committed (see docs/runbook.md,
# "Live eval protocol"). The flat-pointer ignores above (trajectories,
# briefings, traces, latest.json, reports/human) are refreshable by design.
# Not to be confused with `evals/reports/runs/`, which IS ignored above: that
# one holds the per-run aggregate reports, whose durable copy is the archive
# here. Two folders called "runs", opposite rules, on purpose.
#
# Also NOT ignored, and deliberately: evals/reports/baseline/ and
# evals/reports/phase-close/ — the two report families a human commits by
# hand. Their old flat names were tracked and their new folders are too.

# Session context archives (context/README.md). Deliberately NOT committed:
# transcripts carry live credentials, and the raw set is >100MB of binary that
# git would keep forever. context/INDEX.md is the committed part and is the
# part a new session actually reads.
context/archives/*
!context/archives/.gitkeep
